//! The one credential that crosses a host boundary in Ferrite SSO.
//!
//! An assertion says: *this realm's broker verified this subject, for this one
//! audience, until this instant*. It is not a session. It is redeemed once,
//! immediately, for a session the receiving application mints itself.
//!
//! # Why it is signed rather than plain
//!
//! The obvious design hands the subject back on the query string. That is a
//! total authentication bypass: anyone may type
//! `https://docs.example/_sso/callback?subject=someone-else` and become them.
//! The MAC is what makes the browser a courier rather than an author.
//!
//! # What the MAC covers, and why each field is in it
//!
//! * `subject` — the identity being asserted.
//! * `audience` — the one application allowed to redeem it. Without this,
//!   an assertion minted for a low-value property is replayable against a
//!   high-value one, so every property inherits the weakest property's
//!   exposure.
//! * `expires_at_ms` — outside the MAC an attacker simply rewrites it.
//! * `nonce` — makes each assertion distinct, and is the key a receiver
//!   stores to enforce single use.
//!
//! # Threat model, stated honestly
//!
//! The key is symmetric and shared by the broker and every client, so any
//! holder can mint for any audience. A compromised client can therefore forge
//! an assertion to another client. That is acceptable only because every
//! participant here is a first-party property under one operator; it would not
//! be acceptable for tenant applications. Asymmetric signing is the upgrade
//! path, and it needs a primitive the application SDK does not expose today.
//!
//! Replay within the TTL is prevented by the receiver, not by this type: a MAC
//! cannot know whether it has been seen before. [`Assertion::verify`] is
//! therefore necessary but not sufficient — [`crate::ferrite_sso::client`] pairs it with a
//! single-use nonce record.

